1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,250
Does task success mean good teamwork?

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:07,590
I built an AgentWorld experiment: five scripted roles crafting a pickaxe, an axe,

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:08,350
and a sword.

4
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:13,650
I tested normal delivery, a coal shortage, and that shortage plus a delayed request.

5
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:15,190
Here's the third case.

6
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:17,630
I deliver five coal instead of ten.

7
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,610
The smelter runs out and requests more.

8
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,150
I delay that message by three rounds.

9
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:27,010
Once it arrives, the supplier sends the rest, and work resumes.

10
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:30,050
Five runs per condition, all successful.

11
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:35,410
But average completion takes five rounds normally, versus nine with both disruptions.

12
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:40,670
My takeaway: evaluate handoffs and recovery alongside success.

13
00:00:40,860 --> 00:00:44,950
Traces show what happened; they don't reveal a model's internal reasoning.

14
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:47,970
This scripted baseline tests the measurement setup.

15
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:51,430
Next, I'll compare LLM policies.

16
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:54,270
Code and traces are linked in the post.
